arch, writing, and rhetorical practices employed in academic and professional contexts. The course examines the operation of genres, the audiences they address, and the purposes they serve. The course focuses on the analysis and development of student writing and rhetorical practice. Assignments include research essays, digital compositions, and oral presentations.&quot;, &quot;credits&quot;: &quot;4&quot;, &quot;grading&quot;: &quot;Graded (GRD)&quot;, &quot;typically_offered&quot;: &quot;Fall, Spring, Summer&quot;, &quot;requisites&quot;: &quot;ENG 105 non-Z Requisite&quot;, &quot;flexible&quot;: false, &quot;also_ubc&quot;: true}\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"box-eyebrow\"><span class=\"box-eyebrow-tag box-ubc-tag\">UB Curriculum</span></div><div class=\"box-label\"><span class=\"box-code\">ENG 105LEC</span><span class=\"box-title\">Writing and Rhetoric</span></div><span class=\"box-credits\">4 cr</span><div class=\"box-note\">(may also be completed in a different semester shown)</div></div><div class=\"or-sep\">or</div><div class=\"box box-generic\" id=\"economics-ba-ma__box-1-4-1\" data-detail=\"{&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;generic&quot;, &quot;category&quot;: &quot;ubc&quot;, &quot;description&quot;: &quot;Pathway: a UB Curriculum Thematic or Global Pathway course - part of at least four UB Areas/SUNY General Education categories a student&#x27;s Pathways coursework must satisfy across two Pathways (one Thematic, one Global).&quot;, &quot;catalog_url&quot;: &quot;https://www.buffalo.edu/ubcurriculum/pathways.html&quot;}\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"box-eyebrow\"><span class=\"box-eyebrow-tag box-ubc-tag\">UB Curriculum</span></div><div class=\"box-label\">Pathway</div><span class=\"box-credits\">3 cr</span></div></div></div></div>\n        <div class=\"col-total\">16 or 17 credits</div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"semester-col term-fall\">\n        <div class=\"col-header\"><span class=\"col-year\">Year 2</span><span class=\"col-term\">Fall</span></div>\n        <div class=\"col-slots\"><div class=\"slot\"><div class=\"box box-generic\" id=\"economics-ba-ma__box-2-0-0\" data-detail=\"{&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;generic&quot;, &quot;category&quot;: &quot;ubc&quot;, &quot;description&quot;: &quot;Pathway: a UB Curriculum Thematic or Global Pathway course - part of at least four UB Areas/SUNY General Education categories a student&#x27;s Pathways coursework must satisfy across two Pathways (one Thematic, one Global).&quot;, &quot;catalog_url&quot;: &quot;https://www.buffalo.edu/ubcurriculum/pathways.html&quot;}\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"box-eyebrow\"><span class=\"box-eyebrow-tag box-ubc-tag\">UB Curriculum</span></div><div class=\"box-label\">Pathway</div><span class=\"box-credits\">3 cr</span></div></div><div class=\"slot\"><div class=\"box box-course box-flexible\" id=\"economics-ba-ma__box-2-1-0\" data-detail=\"{&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;generic&quot;, &quot;category&quot;: &quot;major_elective&quot;, &quot;description&quot;: &quot;\\&quot;Elective\\&quot; is an elective slot specific to this major&#x27;s own requirements, not a UB Curriculum-wide component - the exact eligible courses depend on this program&#x27;s requirements as listed in the Undergraduate Catalog.&quot;, &quot;catalog_url&quot;: &quot;https://catalogs.buffalo.edu/preview_program.php?catoid=21&amp;poid=7958&amp;returnto=1177&quot;}\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"box-eyebrow\"><span class=\"box-eyebrow-tag\">Elective in the major</span></div><div class=\"box-label\">Elective</div><span class=\"box-credits\">3 cr</span></div></div><div class=\"slot\"><div class=\"box box-generic\" id=\"economics-ba-ma__box-2-2-0\" data-detail=\"{&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;generic&quot;, &quot;category&quot;: &quot;ubc&quot;, &quot;description&quot;: &quot;Scientific Literacy and Inquiry: a 7-credit UB Curriculum Foundations requirement (two lecture courses plus a matching 1-credit lab from the same subject area).&quot;, &quot;catalog_url&quot;: &quot;https://www.buffalo.edu/ubcurriculum/foundations.html&quot;}\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"box-eyebrow\"><span class=\"box-eyebrow-tag box-ubc-tag\">UB Curriculum</span></div><div class=\"box-label\">Scientific Literacy and Inquiry Lab</div><span class=\"box-credits\">1 cr</span></div></div><div class=\"slot\"><div class=\"box box-generic\" id=\"economics-ba-ma__box-2-3-0\" data-detail=\"{&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;generic&quot;, &quot;category&quot;: &quot;ubc&quot;, &quot;description&quot;: &quot;Scientific Literacy and Inquiry: a 7-credit UB Curriculum Foundations requirement (two lecture courses plus a matching 1-credit lab from the same subject area).&quot;, &quot;catalog_url&quot;: &quot;https://www.buffalo.edu/ubcurriculum/foundations.html&quot;}\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"box-eyebrow\"><span class=\"box-eyebrow-tag box-ubc-tag\">UB Curriculum</span></div><div class=\"box-label\">Scientific Literacy and Inquiry 2</div><span class=\"box-credits\">3 cr</span></div></div><div class=\"slot\"><div class=\"or-group\"><div class=\"box box-course\" id=\"economics-ba-ma__box-2-4-0\" data-detail=\"{&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;course&quot;, &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Microeconomic Theory 1&quot;, &quot;description&quot;: &quot;This course presents the core analytical tools that economists use to model the behavior of consumers, firms, and markets. Topics include the analysis of supply and demand, consumer and producer behavior, competitive markets, monopoly, and pricing with market power. The concepts covered in this course underlie the methodology used in nearly all subfields of economics and provide a framework that can be used to analyze many practical decision problems and policy issues. Following completion of this course, students will be prepared to take upper-division elective courses in economics.&quot;, &quot;credits&quot;: &quot;3&quot;, &quot;grading&quot;: &quot;Graded (GRD)&quot;, &quot;typically_offered&quot;: &quot;Fall, Spring, Summer, Winter&quot;, &quot;requisites&quot;: &quot;Pre-Requisite: MTH 121 or MTH 131 or MTH 141 or MTH 136 and MTH 137&quot;, &quot;flexible&quot;: false, &quot;also_ubc&quot;: false}\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"box-label\"><span class=\"box-code\">ECO 405LEC</span><span class=\"box-title\">Microeconomic Theory 1</span></div><span class=\"box-credits\">3 cr</span><div class=\"box-note\">(may also be completed in a different semester shown)</div></div><div class=\"or-sep\">or</div><div class=\"box box-course\" id=\"economics-ba-ma__box-2-4-1\" data-detail=\"{&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;course&quot;, &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Macroeconomic Theory&quot;, &quot;description&quot;: &quot;This course is an intermediate-level examination of the sometimes-competing models of the macroeconomy. It uses economic theory to explain the causes of inflation, business fluctuations, unemployment, and economic growth.&quot;, &quot;credits&quot;: &quot;3&quot;, &quot;grading&quot;: &quot;Graded (GRD)&quot;, &quot;typically_offered&quot;: &quot;Fall, Spring, Summer, Winter&quot;, &quot;requisites&quot;: &quot;Pre-Requisite: MTH 121 or MTH 131 or MTH 141 or MTH 136 and MTH 137&quot;, &quot;flexible&quot;: false, &quot;also_ubc&quot;: false}\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"box-label\"><span class=\"box-code\">ECO 407LEC</span><span class=\"box-title\">Macroeconomic Theory</span></div><span class=\"box-credits\">3 cr</span><div class=\"box-note\">(may also be completed in a different semester shown)</div></div></div></div><div class=\"slot\"><div class=\"box box-course\" id=\"economics-ba-ma__box-2-5-0\" data-detail=\"{&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;course&quot;, &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Economic Statistics and Data Analysis&quot;, &quot;description&quot;: &quot;This course introduces data analysis and econometrics. Econometrics is a collection of methods that use data and statistical techniques to extract information from data, to answer real-world applied questions, and to test predictions of economic theory. Econometrics is used by governments and businesses to study the effects of government policies and to use historical data to forecast future values of variables such as gross domestic product, interest rates, unemployment rates, stock market prices, and sales growth. This course is the first in a sequence on applied economic statistics, econometrics, and data analysis. The focus is on applications and interpreting the findings of applied econometric studies.&quot;, &quot;credits&quot;: &quot;3&quot;, &quot;grading&quot;: &quot;Graded (GRD)&quot;, &quot;typically_offered&quot;: &quot;Fall, Spring, Summer, Winter&quot;, &quot;requisites&quot;: null, &quot;flexible&quot;: false, &quot;also_ubc&quot;: false}\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"box-label\"><span class=\"box-code\">ECO 380LEC</span><span class=\"box-title\">Economic Statistics and Data Analysis</span></div><span class=\"box-credits\">3 cr</span></div></div></div>\n        <div class=\"col-total\">16 credits</div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"semester-col term-spring\">\n        <div class=\"col-header\"><span class=\"col-year\">Year 2</span><span class=\"col-term\">Spring</span></div>\n        <div class=\"col-slots\"><div class=\"slot\"><div class=\"box box-generic\" id=\"economics-ba-ma__box-3-0-0\" data-detail=\"{&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;generic&quot;, &quot;category&quot;: &quot;ubc&quot;, &quot;description&quot;: &quot;Pathway: a UB Curriculum Thematic or Global Pathway course - part of at least four UB Areas/SUNY General Education categories a student&#x27;s Pathways coursework must satisfy across two Pathways (one Thematic, one Global).&quot;, &quot;catalog_url&quot;: &quot;https://www.buffalo.edu/ubcurriculum/pathways.html&quot;}\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"box-eyebrow\"><span class=\"box-eyebrow-tag box-ubc-tag\">UB Curriculum</span></div><div class=\"box-label\">Pathway</div><span class=\"box-credits\">3 cr</span></div></div><div class=\"slot\"><div class=\"box box-generic\" id=\"economics-ba-ma__box-3-1-0\" data-detail=\"{&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;generic&quot;, &quot;category&quot;: &quot;ubc&quot;, &quot;description&quot;: &quot;Pathway: a UB Curriculum Thematic or Global Pathway course - part of at least four UB Areas/SUNY General Education categories a student&#x27;s Pathways coursework must satisfy across two Pathways (one Thematic, one Global).&quot;, &quot;catalog_url&quot;: &quot;https://www.buffalo.edu/ubcurriculum/pathways.html&quot;}\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"box-eyebrow\"><span class=\"box-eyebrow-tag box-ubc-tag\">UB Curriculum</span></div><div class=\"box-label\">Pathway</div><span class=\"box-credits\">3 cr</span></div></div><div class=\"slot\"><div class=\"box box-course box-flexible\" id=\"economics-ba-ma__box-3-2-0\" data-detail=\"{&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;generic&quot;, &quot;category&quot;: &quot;major_elective&quot;, &quot;description&quot;: &quot;\\&quot;Elective\\&quot; is an elective slot specific to this major&#x27;s own requirements, not a UB Curriculum-wide component - the exact eligible courses depend on this program&#x27;s requirements as listed in the Undergraduate Catalog.&quot;, &quot;catalog_url&quot;: &quot;https://catalogs.buffalo.edu/preview_program.php?catoid=21&amp;poid=7958&amp;returnto=1177&quot;}\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"box-eyebrow\"><span class=\"box-eyebrow-tag\">Elective in the major</span></div><div class=\"box-label\">Elective</div><span class=\"box-credits\">3 cr</span></div></div><div class=\"slot\"><div class=\"or-group\"><div class=\"box box-course\" id=\"economics-ba-ma__box-3-3-0\" data-detail=\"{&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;course&quot;, &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Macroeconomic Theory&quot;, &quot;description&quot;: &quot;This course is an intermediate-level examination of the sometimes-competing models of the macroeconomy. It uses economic theory to explain the causes of inflation, business fluctuations, unemployment, and economic growth.&quot;, &quot;credits&quot;: &quot;3&quot;, &quot;grading&quot;: &quot;Graded (GRD)&quot;, &quot;typically_offered&quot;: &quot;Fall, Spring, Summer, Winter&quot;, &quot;requisites&quot;: &quot;Pre-Requisite: MTH 121 or MTH 131 or MTH 141 or MTH 136 and MTH 137&quot;, &quot;flexible&quot;: false, &quot;also_ubc&quot;: false}\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"box-label\"><span class=\"box-code\">ECO 407LEC</span><span class=\"box-title\">Macroeconomic Theory</span></div><span class=\"box-credits\">3 cr</span><div class=\"box-note\">(may also be completed in a different semester shown)</div></div><div class=\"or-sep\">or</div><div class=\"box box-course\" id=\"economics-ba-ma__box-3-3-1\" data-detail=\"{&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;course&quot;, &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Microeconomic Theory 1&quot;, &quot;description&quot;: &quot;This course presents the core analytical tools that economists use to model the behavior of consumers, firms, and markets. Topics include the analysis of supply and demand, consumer and producer behavior, competitive markets, monopoly, and pricing with market power. The concepts covered in this course underlie the methodology used in nearly all subfields of economics and provide a framework that can be used to analyze many practical decision problems and policy issues. Following completion of this course, students will be prepared to take upper-division elective courses in economics.&quot;, &quot;credits&quot;: &quot;3&quot;, &quot;grading&quot;: &quot;Graded (GRD)&quot;, &quot;typically_offered&quot;: &quot;Fall, Spring, Summer, Winter&quot;, &quot;requisites&quot;: &quot;Pre-Requisite: MTH 121 or MTH 131 or MTH 141 or MTH 136 and MTH 137&quot;, &quot;flexible&quot;: false, &quot;also_ubc&quot;: false}\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\"><div class=\"box-label\"><span class=\"box-code\">ECO 405LEC</span><span class=\"box-title\">Microeconomic Theory 1</span></div><span class=\"box-credits\">3 cr</span><div class=\"box-note\">(may also be completed in a different semester shown)</div></div></div></div><div class=\"slot\"><div class=\"box box-course\" id=\"economics-ba-ma__box-3-4-0\" data-detail=\"{&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;course&quot;, &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Econometrics I&quot;, &quot;description&quot;: &quot;This course is the first class on basic applied econometrics, introducing students to the fundamental concepts and techniques used to analyze data. The course focuses on the multiple linear regression models and their assumptions, estimation techniques, and statistical inferences. Econometrics is a tool that allows one to use data and statistical techniques to answer real-world questions. Econometrics is used in all social science settings. For example, economists use econometrics to test predictions of economic theory or conduct policy analysis, governments use econometrics to study the effects of government policies, and businesses use historical data to forecast sales growth. This course emphasizes practical applications and interpreting the findings of applied econometric studies. One essential learning outcome of the class is estimating econometric models using actual data. Therefore, an important part of the class will be the use of Stata, a versatile and powerful computer statistical package used to analyze data. Knowledge of econometrics is valuable for many types of jobs; therefore, knowing a software package such as Stata is also extremely valuable.&quot;, &quot;credits&quot;: &quot;3&quot;, &quot;grading&quot;: &quot;Graded (GRD)&quot;, &quot;typically_offered&quot;: &quot;Fall, Spring, Summer&quot;, &quot;requisites&quot;: &quot;Pre-Requisites:  ECO 380 or GEO 211 or CIE 308 or (MTH 411 and MTH 412 ) or (STA 301 and STA 301 and STA 302 and STA 302 ) or (MTH 411 and STA 302 and STA 302 ) or (STA 301 and STA 301 and MTH 412 ).&quot;, &quot;flexible&quot;: false, &quot;also_ubc&quot;: false}\" rol